<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cologne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cologne is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded as a Roman colony and an important centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s Rhine frontier. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its archbishop was one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It was also one of the largest and wealthiest cities of medieval Germany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cologne.jpg" alt="Cologne" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kranh%C3%A4user_Cologne,_April_2018_-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 496, traditionally at Tolbiac (Zülpich) near <b>Cologne</b>, the Alemanni fought Clovis I; Gregory of Tours tells how Clovis, close to defeat, vowed to become a Christian if he won, and how the Alemanni submitted once their king had been killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Wittelsbachs repeated partitions weakened the duchy for two and a half centuries, until the Landshut War of Succession ended with the <b>Cologne</b> arbitration of 1505, which reunited most of the lands under Albert IV, though the Palatinate-Neuburg was carved out for his rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…came under John I, who in the War of the Limburg Succession defeated Siegfried of Westerburg, archbishop of <b>Cologne</b>, and Count Reinald I of Guelders at the Battle of Worringen on 5 June 1288, and so acquired the Duchy of Limburg and control of the trade route from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Henry was condemned, and in 1180 the Gelnhausen charter divided Saxony: the western part became the Duchy of Westphalia, granted to the archbishop of <b>Cologne</b>, and the ducal title with the eastern lands went to Bernhard of Anhalt, a son of Albert the Bear of the Ascanian family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1672 France, England, Münster and <b>Cologne</b> attacked together, and the Republic survived by flooding the Holland Water Line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In Lotharingia it was held in the eleventh century by the Ezzonid family, with lands around Aachen and <b>Cologne</b>, but pressure from the archbishops of Cologne pushed the counts palatine southwards to the middle Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
